1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,620
and help us give you that space to work on us so that we can become more like you.

2
00:00:05,620 --> 00:00:11,020
Please bless Nick and have him say the things you want him to say, God,

3
00:00:11,020 --> 00:00:14,940
and help open our ears so that we hear the things we really need to hear.

4
00:00:14,940 --> 00:00:21,340
And give us patience with ourselves and each other as we go through this practice.

5
00:00:21,340 --> 00:00:29,400
Thank you for all that you do for us and the promises you keep, even when we don't deserve it.

6
00:00:29,820 --> 00:00:32,000
In Jesus' name we pray. Amen.

7
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:53,660
Solitude is not a private, therapeutic place.

8
00:00:53,660 --> 00:00:59,700
Rather, it is the place of conversion.

9
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:08,840
The place where the old self dies and the new self is born.

10
00:01:08,840 --> 00:01:16,880
Solitude is the furnace of transformation.

11
00:01:21,880 --> 00:01:31,060
Without solitude, we remain victims of our society and continue to be entangled in the illusion of the false self.

12
00:01:31,060 --> 00:01:43,020
Solitude is the place of the great struggle and the great encounter.

13
00:01:43,020 --> 00:01:52,200
Wow.

14
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:53,200
Am I on?

15
00:01:53,200 --> 00:01:53,320
Am I on?

16
00:01:53,320 --> 00:01:54,700
Awesome.

17
00:01:54,700 --> 00:01:55,400
Okay.

18
00:01:55,540 --> 00:02:05,040
let's just sit here for a moment and let's, I just want us to reflect briefly just on the songs that we sang and if you can remember any of the lyrics.

19
00:02:05,040 --> 00:02:15,920
And so I don't know if that was strategic in choosing these songs, but this is the, you couldn't have chosen better songs to walk into what it means to walk into solitude.

20
00:02:16,720 --> 00:02:30,100
Especially when we look at our, let's just sit here and pause for a moment in silent prayer as a, before we enter into today's teaching.

21
00:02:30,100 --> 00:02:51,500
Jesus, I pray that you would reveal everything that needs to be revealed today and that we would have eyes to see and ears to hear.

22
00:02:51,500 --> 00:02:54,780
Holy Spirit, we bless you in your name.

23
00:02:55,420 --> 00:02:55,680
Amen.

24
00:02:55,680 --> 00:03:07,940
So in the fall of 2020, we were sort of in a, as a globe, as a collective, you know, not even just the body of Christ, the entire world were in a pandemic.

25
00:03:07,940 --> 00:03:14,800
And then as I later began to see it as a forced time of solitude.

26
00:03:15,760 --> 00:03:28,280
And I had been on a journey of what Martin Laird titled his outstanding book called Into the Silent Land, meaning that the inward journey of prayer and identity had taken center stage.

27
00:03:28,280 --> 00:03:38,080
Fast forward nine months from that into May of 2021, and I walked into a three-day experience that was incredibly challenging.

28
00:03:38,220 --> 00:03:45,340
It was life-giving and deeply impactful, and it also left me with a deep desire for more.

29
00:03:45,340 --> 00:03:57,720
Just down the road from here, off Route 7, there's, nestled off the Shenandoah River, a couple hundred yards, is a Trappist monastery called Holy Cross Abbey.

30
00:03:58,660 --> 00:04:05,560
And this was, for me, the first time I would go on a silent retreat.

31
00:04:05,560 --> 00:04:08,360
No phones, no talking.

32
00:04:08,360 --> 00:04:15,440
I did take a Bible and a book, although those things, reading is not preferred.

33
00:04:16,520 --> 00:04:29,780
What I would learn in this very short three days is that I talk entirely too much, I read more than I sit and just be with God, and I definitely do not get enough sleep.

34
00:04:29,780 --> 00:04:37,740
The point or goal of a silent retreat is to remove distractions, and some call it entering into the desert.

35
00:04:37,740 --> 00:04:41,240
The desert is a place where we shed things.

36
00:04:42,680 --> 00:04:44,760
And think of Jesus.

37
00:04:44,760 --> 00:04:48,980
Jesus entered the desert after he was baptized, or think of Moses or Elijah.

38
00:04:48,980 --> 00:04:56,520
Each have stories of where they entered a desert at a pivotal time in their life, and they didn't go there to gain something.

39
00:04:56,520 --> 00:05:05,240
The desert doesn't teach us things like acquiring more knowledge by reading or listening to a podcast or some new cool TED talk.

40
00:05:05,240 --> 00:05:16,580
The desert teaches us by subtraction, by taking away our props, our distractions, leaving our souls naked and exposed and raw.

41
00:05:16,580 --> 00:05:21,420
The truth is, is that we're all really naked and exposed before the Lord.

42
00:05:21,420 --> 00:05:30,400
We just distract ourselves with fig leaves, thinking that we're hiding, when we merely are covering up our own selves.

43
00:05:30,920 --> 00:05:34,520
Henry Nguyen, in his outstanding book, The Way of the Heart, he says this,

44
00:05:34,520 --> 00:05:37,840
In solitude is not a private, therapeutic place.

45
00:05:37,840 --> 00:05:40,600
Rather, it's a place of conversation.

46
00:05:40,600 --> 00:05:45,660
The place where the old self goes to die, and the new self is born.

47
00:05:45,660 --> 00:05:50,580
The place where the emergence of the new man or the new woman occurs.

48
00:05:50,580 --> 00:05:54,040
In solitude, I get rid of the scaffolding.

49
00:05:54,740 --> 00:05:56,620
I have no friends to talk with.

50
00:05:56,620 --> 00:05:58,940
I have no telephone calls to make.

51
00:05:58,940 --> 00:06:01,060
I have no meetings to attend.

52
00:06:01,060 --> 00:06:02,940
No music to entertain.

53
00:06:02,940 --> 00:06:04,880
No books to distract.

54
00:06:04,880 --> 00:06:06,420
Just me.

55
00:06:06,420 --> 00:06:12,340
Naked, vulnerable, weak, sinful, deprived, broken.

56
00:06:12,340 --> 00:06:13,700
Nothing.

57
00:06:13,700 --> 00:06:17,900
It is in this nothingness that I have to come face.

58
00:06:18,040 --> 00:06:25,980
I have to face my solitude, a nothingness so dreadful that everything in me wants to run to my friends, my work, my distractions.

59
00:06:25,980 --> 00:06:30,140
Solitude is the furnace of transformation.

60
00:06:30,140 --> 00:06:34,600
The place of great struggle and great encounter.

61
00:06:34,600 --> 00:06:39,100
The struggle against the compulsion of the false self.

62
00:06:39,100 --> 00:06:51,760
Henry absolutely nails the reality of what solitude is.

63
00:06:51,760 --> 00:06:58,620
It's the encounter with the loving God who offers himself as the substance of the new self.

64
00:06:58,620 --> 00:07:02,380
Think of Ephesians 4, 24, where Paul says,

65
00:07:02,380 --> 00:07:14,200
Last week, you started here the solitude practice.

66
00:07:14,200 --> 00:07:18,500
Next week, you're going to walk into an encounter with your enemy.

67
00:07:18,500 --> 00:07:23,800
The week after that, when it closes, you're going to walk into an encounter with our God.

68
00:07:23,800 --> 00:07:29,040
But today, we're going to do a really hard one, and that's encounter with ourself.

69
00:07:29,900 --> 00:07:36,020
The first thing you do when you go into solitude, rather than feeling happy or at peace,

70
00:07:36,020 --> 00:07:42,580
the first thing that happens, as Father Thomas Keating sometimes calls,

71
00:07:42,580 --> 00:07:44,440
the unfolding of our unconscious.

72
00:07:44,440 --> 00:07:49,960
Which is a really fancy word of saying that whatever is down must come up.

73
00:07:49,960 --> 00:07:54,920
From the depths of ourselves to surface in our heart.

74
00:07:55,560 --> 00:08:04,820
And we begin to feel things so deep and so buried that we're not really sure or ready for some things that we've buried.

75
00:08:04,820 --> 00:08:08,580
Am I alone in burying things deep within me?

76
00:08:08,580 --> 00:08:11,080
Okay, that's just between my wife and I.

77
00:08:11,080 --> 00:08:16,380
Here's a few common things that come up when you go into solitude.

78
00:08:16,380 --> 00:08:18,860
If you're taking notes, the first thing is exhaustion.

79
00:08:19,880 --> 00:08:22,900
Often, the first thing you feel is just tired.

80
00:08:22,900 --> 00:08:24,720
Some call it a crash.

81
00:08:24,720 --> 00:08:29,680
It's like you've been running on adrenaline, and then all of a sudden, everything runs out,

82
00:08:29,680 --> 00:08:34,040
and even in your bones, you feel this deep sense of weariness.

83
00:08:34,040 --> 00:08:39,980
In the first 30 minutes of my silent retreat, I passed right out.

84
00:08:40,980 --> 00:08:46,020
And then over the next day, I would nap eight or nine more times.

85
00:08:46,020 --> 00:08:50,960
Which really, I started to shame myself.

86
00:08:50,960 --> 00:08:52,280
Like, come on, man, focus up.

87
00:08:52,280 --> 00:08:53,720
Like, you can do better than this.

88
00:08:53,720 --> 00:08:54,380
Like, what are you doing?

89
00:08:54,380 --> 00:08:58,020
And after I got through all the guilt and the shame,

90
00:08:58,020 --> 00:09:02,640
my perspective became more clear in that what I really needed was rest.

91
00:09:02,640 --> 00:09:06,160
That I pushed myself incredibly too hard.

92
00:09:06,160 --> 00:09:10,840
You see, because rest is an essential component in our discipleship to Jesus.

93
00:09:10,840 --> 00:09:14,780
Every one of us in this room, regardless of where you are in life,

94
00:09:14,780 --> 00:09:17,460
you're incredibly good at overscheduling yourself.

95
00:09:17,460 --> 00:09:21,240
I am primary guilt of that.

96
00:09:21,240 --> 00:09:25,160
The Lord is reteaching me rest in this season.

97
00:09:25,160 --> 00:09:28,260
It feels unfortunate to have to relearn these things,

98
00:09:28,260 --> 00:09:33,680
but it is a deep necessity for our longevity and our journey with Jesus.

99
00:09:34,180 --> 00:09:37,860
Think of, like, tiredness on, like, a spectrum, okay?

100
00:09:37,860 --> 00:09:39,760
One side is a good tired.

101
00:09:39,760 --> 00:09:40,880
You worked a long week.

102
00:09:40,880 --> 00:09:42,540
Yet it was very productive.

103
00:09:42,540 --> 00:09:44,560
You feel really good.

104
00:09:44,560 --> 00:09:46,060
You made the world a better place.

105
00:09:46,060 --> 00:09:48,940
You were patient with others and your kids.

106
00:09:48,940 --> 00:09:52,220
You gave your life away healthfully.

107
00:09:52,220 --> 00:09:56,500
And you prepared to rest at the end of the week and maybe even you Sabbathed.

108
00:09:56,500 --> 00:09:59,980
I'm not going to ask anybody to raise hands where you are on the spectrum.

109
00:10:00,600 --> 00:10:04,680
The second one is, then there's an unhealthy tired, where I find myself regularly,

110
00:10:04,680 --> 00:10:08,540
where you didn't sleep enough, high stress, low margin.

111
00:10:08,540 --> 00:10:11,100
It was manageable, but let's be straight.

112
00:10:11,100 --> 00:10:12,760
You were on the edge for most of the week.

113
00:10:12,760 --> 00:10:16,440
And then there's the dangerous kind of tired,

114
00:10:16,440 --> 00:10:19,140
where you lost touch with your soul and God,

115
00:10:19,140 --> 00:10:21,860
making you easy pick off for temptation.

116
00:10:22,560 --> 00:10:25,440
Where would you find yourself on the spectrum?

117
00:10:25,440 --> 00:10:28,480
If you're good tired, rest can keep you healthy.

118
00:10:28,480 --> 00:10:33,100
If you're unhealthy tired, rest can get you back to being healthy.

119
00:10:33,600 --> 00:10:37,940
But if you're in a dangerous space of tiredness,

120
00:10:37,940 --> 00:10:41,440
it can expose a need for some serious change.

121
00:10:41,440 --> 00:10:45,920
I've been finding myself very unhealthily tired recently,

122
00:10:45,920 --> 00:10:48,180
and I'm learning to rest again,

123
00:10:48,180 --> 00:10:56,600
which is how I started with a forced solitude versus an intentional solitude.

124
00:10:57,140 --> 00:11:01,520
It was really easy to do when we were forced to do stuff.

125
00:11:01,520 --> 00:11:07,160
It makes it more incredibly challenging when you have to do it intentionally.

126
00:11:07,160 --> 00:11:11,240
The second thing that comes up when you go into solitude is fear.

127
00:11:12,700 --> 00:11:17,940
Often I find my mind racing and worrying about a thousand different things,

128
00:11:17,940 --> 00:11:20,940
which normally leads to the prolonged challenge

129
00:11:20,940 --> 00:11:23,500
of having a ping pong match with God in my mind.

130
00:11:23,500 --> 00:11:27,120
Bouncing back and forth between things, not sitting down.

131
00:11:27,120 --> 00:11:31,920
Certainly my focus is off because fear has reared its ugly head,

132
00:11:31,920 --> 00:11:35,940
and I just keep getting stuck in this ping pong match.

133
00:11:35,940 --> 00:11:38,560
The third thing that comes up is sadness.

134
00:11:39,320 --> 00:11:43,360
I don't want to sound bleak, but underneath everything in life

135
00:11:43,360 --> 00:11:44,760
is the kind of sadness.

136
00:11:44,760 --> 00:11:46,660
Think about it like this.

137
00:11:46,660 --> 00:11:50,660
Even the moments of happiness, like a graduation or a wedding,

138
00:11:50,660 --> 00:11:54,620
the birth of a child, all those come with a note of sadness.

139
00:11:54,620 --> 00:11:56,280
Like it's the graduation.

140
00:11:56,280 --> 00:11:58,520
Oh, well, I'm going to be missing my friends

141
00:11:58,520 --> 00:12:01,280
because I won't see them every day in high school anymore

142
00:12:01,280 --> 00:12:02,640
because now I've graduated.

143
00:12:02,640 --> 00:12:05,160
There's notes of sadness that stick around.

144
00:12:05,380 --> 00:12:11,220
Sorrow and joy intermix in this life and whether we see it or not.

145
00:12:11,220 --> 00:12:14,700
And when you go into solitude, this is a prime time

146
00:12:14,700 --> 00:12:17,140
for sadness to well up inside of you.

147
00:12:17,140 --> 00:12:20,360
It's like almost your soul has been waiting to grieve.

148
00:12:20,360 --> 00:12:24,220
And it's best to just sit back, sit with God,

149
00:12:24,220 --> 00:12:26,420
and let these things pass through you.

150
00:12:26,420 --> 00:12:29,080
It's not always gray clouds and doom.

151
00:12:29,620 --> 00:12:32,120
The upside is when you let yourself feel sadness,

152
00:12:32,120 --> 00:12:36,040
it often gives way to joy, but it might take time.

153
00:12:36,040 --> 00:12:38,940
The fourth thing that comes up is anger.

154
00:12:38,940 --> 00:12:41,840
All the contemplatives write about this,

155
00:12:41,840 --> 00:12:44,220
about how in quiet prayer,

156
00:12:44,220 --> 00:12:48,100
one of the major challenges that people are faced with

157
00:12:48,100 --> 00:12:49,980
is all your hurts come up.

158
00:12:49,980 --> 00:12:52,120
You're left by yourself.

159
00:12:52,120 --> 00:12:53,160
You're left to think.

160
00:12:53,160 --> 00:12:55,360
You're left to, you know, yourself.

161
00:12:55,500 --> 00:12:57,760
And the things that are deep, like I said in the beginning,

162
00:12:57,760 --> 00:12:59,100
start to well up.

163
00:12:59,100 --> 00:13:03,400
And sometimes there's, you'll find that you start having

164
00:13:03,400 --> 00:13:05,180
those fake conversations with yourself.

165
00:13:05,180 --> 00:13:07,780
And you talk about all the things,

166
00:13:07,780 --> 00:13:10,620
that person that wronged you or cut you off

167
00:13:10,620 --> 00:13:13,820
or any type of bitterness that might be hanging around.

168
00:13:13,820 --> 00:13:15,720
Occasionally, you know,

169
00:13:15,720 --> 00:13:17,920
I find myself ruminating on these things.

170
00:13:17,920 --> 00:13:20,540
And what I've come to find out is perhaps

171
00:13:20,540 --> 00:13:22,880
maybe I haven't forgiven that person

172
00:13:22,880 --> 00:13:25,040
that I'm having that fake conversation with.

173
00:13:25,040 --> 00:13:27,400
Because if I've truly forgiven them,

174
00:13:27,400 --> 00:13:29,380
then I'm not gonna have a fake conversation

175
00:13:29,380 --> 00:13:32,160
arguing my point with somebody that's not even there

176
00:13:32,160 --> 00:13:33,900
or perhaps not even on this earth anymore.

177
00:13:33,900 --> 00:13:36,560
The fifth thing,

178
00:13:36,560 --> 00:13:38,840
the fifth emotion that comes up is shame.

179
00:13:38,840 --> 00:13:41,980
When we're stripped down, naked and exposed,

180
00:13:41,980 --> 00:13:44,320
who we are really comes through.

181
00:13:44,320 --> 00:13:46,740
With other people, we can perform,

182
00:13:46,740 --> 00:13:48,460
we can project an image,

183
00:13:48,460 --> 00:13:50,900
we can attempt to control the narrative

184
00:13:50,900 --> 00:13:53,020
or the perspective that people have of us.

185
00:13:53,020 --> 00:13:56,400
We can even fool ourselves to an extent

186
00:13:56,400 --> 00:13:58,140
into thinking that we're hiding

187
00:13:58,140 --> 00:13:59,520
because we're pretty good at it.

188
00:13:59,520 --> 00:14:01,620
Like I said in the beginning,

189
00:14:01,620 --> 00:14:04,060
we tend to hide behind fig leaves.

190
00:14:04,060 --> 00:14:07,460
But in solitude, there's nowhere to hide.

191
00:14:07,460 --> 00:14:12,340
It's you, the good, the bad, the ugly,

192
00:14:12,340 --> 00:14:15,160
all laid bare before a holy God.

193
00:14:15,840 --> 00:14:18,320
We often feel a sense,

194
00:14:18,320 --> 00:14:21,680
a profound sense of our deep need for salvation.

195
00:14:21,680 --> 00:14:25,640
If I could reduce all this into one word,

196
00:14:25,640 --> 00:14:27,800
I would call it pain.

197
00:14:27,800 --> 00:14:32,100
In solitude, we encounter pain.

198
00:14:32,100 --> 00:14:37,200
Existential philosopher Siren Korkagard says

199
00:14:37,200 --> 00:14:39,860
that there's two types of people in the world.

200
00:14:39,860 --> 00:14:45,840
There are those that are in despair and know it

201
00:14:45,840 --> 00:14:49,180
and those that are in despair and don't know it.

202
00:14:49,180 --> 00:14:53,000
Inside all of us is a deep well of pain,

203
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:56,500
sometimes stored up only to be released

204
00:14:56,500 --> 00:14:58,980
at a church picnic or a family reunion.

205
00:14:58,980 --> 00:15:05,400
Remember, in solitude, what goes down must come up.

206
00:15:05,400 --> 00:15:07,800
No matter how extroverted you are

207
00:15:07,800 --> 00:15:11,360
or sunny your disposition or robust your faith,

208
00:15:11,360 --> 00:15:12,120
there's pain.

209
00:15:12,120 --> 00:15:15,600
You might be able to mask it better,

210
00:15:15,600 --> 00:15:17,020
but there's pain.

211
00:15:17,020 --> 00:15:20,220
Why are we touching on this subject?

212
00:15:20,220 --> 00:15:25,840
Why would we stir up dormant pain in our lives?

213
00:15:25,840 --> 00:15:27,860
Because things can leak.

214
00:15:27,860 --> 00:15:32,920
Hopefully, not like toxic pollution on everyone around us,

215
00:15:33,400 --> 00:15:34,820
but if we're being honest,

216
00:15:34,820 --> 00:15:36,780
those who are closest to us

217
00:15:36,780 --> 00:15:39,580
often get the best and worst of us

218
00:15:39,580 --> 00:15:41,900
and we can leak things out.

219
00:15:41,900 --> 00:15:43,840
To get free from something,

220
00:15:43,840 --> 00:15:46,420
we must face the thing.

221
00:15:46,420 --> 00:15:50,240
One of our biggest issues facing our culture today

222
00:15:50,240 --> 00:15:52,500
is distractions.

223
00:15:52,500 --> 00:15:55,940
More so, I think, than in the past,

224
00:15:55,940 --> 00:15:57,600
but it's really our cultural moment.

225
00:15:57,600 --> 00:16:00,000
We have our distractions of our day,

226
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:01,940
not some of the distractions

227
00:16:01,940 --> 00:16:04,540
that when early contemplatives were writing

228
00:16:04,540 --> 00:16:06,180
about this in the third century,

229
00:16:06,180 --> 00:16:08,820
their distractions were incredibly different,

230
00:16:08,820 --> 00:16:11,620
but they're probably close to the same.

231
00:16:11,620 --> 00:16:17,460
We do a good job of not feeling pain.

232
00:16:17,460 --> 00:16:22,120
We do a good job of not talking about our pain.

233
00:16:22,120 --> 00:16:24,580
And I'm not talking about taking painkillers

234
00:16:24,580 --> 00:16:25,680
because you have a broken leg.

235
00:16:26,180 --> 00:16:28,360
I'm talking about emotional pain

236
00:16:28,360 --> 00:16:30,460
that we choose to hide from.

237
00:16:30,460 --> 00:16:32,580
We inoculate ourselves

238
00:16:32,580 --> 00:16:34,560
with our distractions of choice,

239
00:16:34,560 --> 00:16:38,800
food, alcohol, work, travel, social media, internet.

240
00:16:38,800 --> 00:16:41,660
Neil Postman wrote a book in 1985

241
00:16:41,660 --> 00:16:44,520
that's called Amusing Ourselves to Death,

242
00:16:44,520 --> 00:16:47,400
calling attention to our cultural obsession

243
00:16:47,400 --> 00:16:48,520
with entertainment,

